// mecatl driver API: the remote schedule-store protocol.
//
// ScheduleStoreService is the wire seam behind the harness's
// `port.ScheduleStore` (engine/port/schedule.go): an operator-run driver process
// persists scheduled-task schedules (and their fire records) on behalf of the
// harness, in place of the in-process stores (memschedulestore, a future JSONL
// store, a k8s-backed store). The driver sits at the SAME trust tier as an
// on-disk store directory — operator-configured infrastructure, not a
// model-facing surface.
//
// The schedule and fire payloads are OPAQUE to the driver: each is a
// format-tagged JSON envelope (the SessionSnapshot{format, payload} precedent),
// and the payload is exactly the harness's `encoding/json` of a
// `port.Schedule` / `port.ScheduleFire` (the same encoding the in-process
// jsonlstore/redisstore persist). The driver stores and returns the envelope
// VERBATIM and never decodes it — schedule schema evolution lives harness-side
// (additive JSON), while format identification lives in this envelope. The
// harness rejects an unknown `format` on Load/LoadFire with an infrastructure
// error (never not-found).
//
// CAPACITY: a schedule is small (a prompt + a few fields, no inline media —
// the store is the durable registry, not the fire's session content), so it
// needs no raised message cap beyond the server-level gRPC default; the
// envelope payload is well under 4 KiB in practice. (Contrast
// SessionSnapshot's 64 MiB minimum, which exists because a session snapshot may
// carry inline media.)
//
// Driver contract (mirrors port.ScheduleStore):
//   - Load of an unknown schedule name MUST return NOT_FOUND (the harness maps
//     it to port.ErrScheduleNotFound). Delete is IDEMPOTENT: an unknown name is
//     success (a NOT_FOUND from a thin driver is tolerated by the harness
//     client and mapped to success). RecordFire is IDEMPOTENT per fire id:
//     recording the same fire id twice is a no-op (the driver MUST NOT
//     duplicate the fire record on a repeat RecordFire).
//   - Claim / ClaimNow / ReArmOneShot / SetEnabled / RecordFire* carry the
//     SAME atomic-advance semantics as the in-process port.ScheduleStore: the
//     durable NextFireAt advance IS the at-most-once fence, and the harness
//     CLIENT cannot fence over the wire. A conforming driver MUST apply these
//     atomically (a backend with compare-and-swap or a per-key mutex does so
//     natively; a backend that GET-then-SETs without a fence is non-conforming
//     and may double-fire). The wire adds nothing to the atomicity — it is a
//     thin translator over the wrapped backend.
//   - A driver that cannot store schedules at all answers every RPC with
//     UNIMPLEMENTED; the harness client maps that to port.ErrScheduleUnsupported
//     (the sticky-disable sentinel), and composition logs one INFO and
//     stickily disables scheduling for the process (byte-identical no-schedule
//     path).
//
// ScheduleOneShotReArmerService is a SEPARATE service carrying the single
// optional ReArmOneShot RPC (mirroring port.ScheduleOneShotReArmer, an OPTIONAL
// interface type-asserted on a ScheduleStore). A driver that does not implement
// the re-armer answers UNIMPLEMENTED; the harness client maps that to
// port.ErrScheduleUnsupported (the same sticky-disable sentinel), so a driver
// that serves only ScheduleStoreService still degrades gracefully.
//
// Validation: required-field annotations are authored with `buf.validate.field`
// for documentation and future runtime enforcement; V1 enforces them in the Go
// client/server wrappers (internal/adapter/grpcdriver).

